    title: 'Flexible Working and Leave Policy at Meridian Logistics',
    sectionNumber: 2,
    wordCount: 500,
    body: `Meridian Logistics is committed to supporting staff in balancing their professional responsibilities with personal commitments. This section of the staff handbook outlines the company's flexible working arrangements, annual leave entitlements, and the procedure for requesting time off. All permanent employees, regardless of department, are covered by the policies described below; agency and temporary staff should instead consult the separate Temporary Staff Handbook.

Flexible Working Arrangements
Employees who have completed at least six months of continuous service may apply for flexible working, which includes options such as compressed hours, staggered start and finish times, and, for roles that do not require a physical presence in the warehouse, partial remote working. Applications must be submitted in writing to a direct line manager using the Flexible Working Request Form, available from the intranet. Line managers are required to respond within twenty-one days, either approving the request, proposing a modified arrangement, or explaining in writing why the request cannot be accommodated at that time. Employees whose initial request is declined may appeal to the Human Resources department within fourteen days of receiving the decision.

Annual Leave
Full-time employees accrue twenty-five days of paid annual leave per calendar year, in addition to statutory public holidays. Part-time employees accrue leave on a pro-rata basis according to their contracted hours. Leave requests should be submitted through the online scheduling system at least two weeks in advance wherever possible, and no more than ten consecutive working days may be taken without prior written approval from a departmental head. Unused leave may be carried over into the following year only up to a maximum of five days, and any leave beyond this limit is forfeited unless the employee was unable to take it due to approved sick leave or maternity or paternity leave.

Sick Leave and Return-to-Work Procedure
Employees who are unable to attend work due to illness must notify their line manager by telephone before their scheduled start time, rather than by text message or email. For absences lasting more than seven consecutive calendar days, a medical certificate from a general practitioner is required. Upon returning to work after any period of sickness absence, employees are required to complete a brief return-to-work interview with their line manager, the purpose of which is to record the reason for absence, confirm the employee's fitness to resume duties, and identify whether any workplace adjustments would help prevent similar absences in future.

Parental Leave
Employees expecting to take maternity, paternity, adoption, or shared parental leave should inform their line manager and the Human Resources department as early as reasonably possible, and no later than fifteen weeks before the expected date, to allow sufficient time for planning cover arrangements. The company offers an enhanced parental pay scheme for employees with more than two years of continuous service at the time leave commences, details of which are set out in a separate policy document available from Human Resources.

Training and Professional Development Scheme
All employees are entitled to apply for the Meridian Development Fund, which reimburses up to £600 per year towards approved external courses, professional qualifications, or conference attendance directly relevant to the employee's current role or a realistic future role within the company. Applications must be approved in advance by a line manager and countersigned by a departmental head before any course is booked; retrospective claims for courses already completed will not be reimbursed under any circumstances.`,
