    id: '22000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005',
    ieltsType: 'general',
    title: 'Warehouse Safety Induction Handbook: Cedar Distribution',
    sectionNumber: 2,
    wordCount: 480,
    body: `All new employees and agency staff working within Cedar Distribution's warehouse facilities must complete this safety induction before beginning any warehouse-based duties, regardless of previous experience gained elsewhere. This handbook summarises the key safety procedures; a full copy of the Site Safety Manual is available from the Health and Safety Officer and should be consulted for any procedure not covered here.

Personal Protective Equipment
High-visibility vests, safety footwear with reinforced toecaps, and hearing protection in designated high-noise zones are mandatory at all times within the warehouse floor, without exception. Hard hats are required only in areas marked with yellow-and-black signage, typically close to the racking bays where stock is loaded and unloaded above head height. Staff who repeatedly fail to wear required protective equipment will be removed from warehouse duties pending a formal review with their supervisor.

Forklift and Machinery Operation
Only employees holding a current, site-specific forklift licence, issued after completing Cedar Distribution's internal training course, may operate powered industrial trucks. This licence must be renewed every three years through a half-day refresher course. Pedestrians must always give way to moving forklifts and should never walk through a racking aisle while a forklift's amber warning light is flashing. Any employee who witnesses a near-miss involving a forklift, whether or not anyone was injured, is required to report it using the Incident Report Form within twenty-four hours.

Manual Handling
Employees expected to lift loads regularly as part of their role must complete a manual handling training session before starting work, and again every two years thereafter as a refresher. As a general guide, no individual should attempt to lift an item weighing more than 25 kilograms without mechanical assistance or the help of a second person; heavier items must be moved using a trolley, pallet truck, or forklift. Employees who experience back pain or discomfort after lifting should report this to their supervisor immediately rather than continuing to work through it.

Fire Safety and Emergency Procedures
Fire extinguishers are located at intervals throughout the warehouse and are colour-coded according to the type of fire they are designed to tackle; staff are not expected to memorise these codes but should never attempt to fight a fire that is spreading beyond its point of origin. In the event of a fire alarm, all staff must proceed immediately to their designated assembly point in the staff car park and must not re-enter the building until a manager confirms it is safe to do so. Fire drills are conducted twice per year, and attendance is compulsory for all staff on shift at the time.

Reporting Hazards
Any employee who identifies a potential hazard, such as a spillage, damaged racking, or blocked fire exit, should report it immediately to a supervisor and, where safe to do so, place a warning cone or sign near the hazard while awaiting a permanent fix. Hazards should also be logged in the Hazard Register kept at the site office, which is reviewed weekly by the Health and Safety Officer.`,
